{
  "dom_id": "step-e2279",
  "name": "Example: Calculating an Amount Given a Ratio",
  "problem": "In a ballet school, the ratio of the number of students with black hair to the total number of students is $5: 8.$ If there are $25$ students with black hair, how many students are there in ballet school who don't have black hair?",
  "worked_solution": "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}8}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}5}}{{\\color{red}8}}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of students with black hair and the denominator represents the total number of students.\n\nWe know that there are $25$ students with black hair, so we want to make a numerator of $25.$ We can do this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}5}\\times 5}{{\\color{red}8}\\times 5} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}25}}{{\\color{red}40}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since there are $25$ students with black hair, there must be $40$ students at the ballet school. Of this amount, the number of students who do not have black hair is\n\n$$\n40 - 25 = 15.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
